text:
Both problems are real.

**C1: confirmed.** `app/visits.py:13` calls `datetime.fromtimestamp(ts)` without a `tz` argument, so Python converts each timestamp to the server's local time. But the docstring on line 12 says the function counts UTC days, and `day_start` on line 7 works in UTC. On a server that isn't set to UTC, visits near midnight land on the wrong day. That gives wrong counts, so I rate it blocking. The fix is `datetime.fromtimestamp(ts, tz=timezone.utc)`.

**C2: confirmed.** The test at `tests/test_visits.py:11` compares only `.values()`, never the date keys. All three timestamps are at noon UTC, plus or minus a minute. Real timezone offsets run from −12 to +14 hours, so any shift moves all three visits together and keeps the 2-and-1 grouping. The keys would change, but the test doesn't look at them. So the test would pass with the C1 bug. Checking the full dict, e.g. `{"2026-10-10": 2, "2026-10-11": 1}`, and adding timestamps near midnight would catch it.

